<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Denbighshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Denbighshire is a county in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, stretching from the coast at Rhyl and Prestatyn to the hills around Corwen, with its administrative centre at Ruthin. It includes much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-clwyd">Vale of Clwyd</a> and the Clwydian Range, and castles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbigh">Denbigh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhuddlan">Rhuddlan</a> built during Edward I's conquest of Wales. It was created as a shire in 1536 and re-formed in 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denbighshire.jpg" alt="Denbighshire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geir Hval (www.MacWhale.eu) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eglwyseg_(2011.10.16).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
